\name{st.cross.plan.execute}
\alias{st.cross.plan.execute}
\title{Executes Named Families from a Cross Plan}
\description{
Creates the specified families from exact named founders in an existing
simulation population, with equal progeny counts and actual simulated
individual identities and pedigrees.
}
\usage{
st.cross.plan.execute(plan, population, name, scheme = "DH")
}
\arguments{
  \item{plan}{A result from \code{st.cross.plan} containing a nonempty
  \code{crosses} data frame. The columns \code{family}, \code{P1Name},
  \code{P2Name}, and \code{n.progeny} specify the families independently of
  other result components. Family IDs are distinct positive whole numbers.
  The parent names identify individuals in \code{population}. Parent pairs
  are distinct, unordered and non-selfed. Progeny counts are equal positive
  whole numbers; family IDs and counts are numeric rather than factors.}
  \item{population}{One character string naming an existing diploid founder
  population. Each selected founder ID occurs exactly once in this population.
  Selected founders are complete and homozygous at every installed simulation
  marker.}
  \item{name}{One nonempty character string containing only ASCII letters
  and digits. This prefix determines the family populations, in plan-row
  order, as \code{name001}, \code{name002}, and so on. Suffixes have at
  least three digits. All target populations must be absent. The combined
  prefix and suffix leave space within the 255-character population-name
  limit for the internal F1 identity suffix.}
  \item{scheme}{Either \code{"DH"} or \code{"F1"}. For \code{"DH"},
  an F1 is generated from each planned pair and its meiotic products are
  doubled to obtain homozygous progeny. For \code{"F1"}, the offspring
  result directly from the planned parental crosses.}
}
\details{
Simulation initialization occurs before this function.
\code{st.set.simpop} initializes the simulation state from a marker data set
and creates the founder population. That initialization function performs its
own reset; a single initialization can support several subsequent plans.
The simulation random number generator is also initialized with
\code{rng.init} before execution.

The installed simulation genome and marker map determine meiosis. The map
contains markers on every chromosome, with valid chromosome lengths and
positions. Existing crossover settings are retained. The initial execution
scope is diploid line breeding with complete, homozygous selected founders.
Missing marker states and heterozygous selected founders produce an error;
execution does not impute or phase them. Valid chromosome segments flanking
the installed markers remain supported.

Parent names select the actual founders used for each family. The function
does not sample parental pairs from a larger pool. For DH production, one
F1 from the inbred parents supplies the family; independent meioses from this
F1 produce the requested doubled haploids. This corresponds to DH production
without additional intermating generations.

All family counts, selected IDs, unordered pair uniqueness, supported founder
states, and target-name collisions are checked before execution changes the
simulation state. Counts and the total offspring-design size are bounded by
\code{.Machine$integer.max}, even when a planner result permits a larger
produced-progeny budget. Errors or interruptions during execution remove
all populations newly created by that call, including completed families from
the same call. Existing founder and unrelated populations are preserved.
Simulation random-number and identity streams may advance during an
interrupted or failed execution; their earlier states are not restored.

The function preserves the existing genome, map, effects and thread settings.
It does not reset simulation state or change the caller's R random-number
state. The returned offspring names and pedigrees are generated by the
simulation engine from the actual parents and F1, rather than assigned from
an intended pairing after a random cross.
}
\value{
An invisible ordinary data frame with one row per family and columns
\code{family}, \code{population}, \code{P1Name}, \code{P2Name},
\code{n.progeny}, and \code{scheme}. Its ordinary printed display contains
these compact family summaries.

The \code{design} attribute is a data frame with one row per simulated
individual. Its columns are \code{family}, \code{population}, \code{name},
\code{P1Name}, \code{P2Name}, \code{F1}, \code{pedigree}, and \code{scheme}.
\code{name} and \code{pedigree} contain the actual offspring identity and
pedigree. \code{F1} contains the actual intermediate F1 identity for DH
families and \code{NA} for direct F1 production. The family populations remain
available to the other simulation functions.
}
\references{
Maurer HP, Melchinger AE, Frisch M (2008) Population genetic simulation and
data analysis with Plabsoft. Euphytica 161:133--139.
}
\seealso{
\code{st.cross.plan}, \code{st.set.simpop}, \code{rng.init},
\code{population.pedigree}, \code{genotype.population},
\code{evaluate.population}
}